00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
hey guys I'm Abbi short welcome to  Abby's kitchen so last time you saw me  sitting in this chair I was getting  emotional as I was getting ready for my  first Mother's Day it was pre baby and  now of course I'm on the other side  I'm almost seven weeks postpartum so  that's really exciting and I mean this  journey is being so interesting and  overwhelming and one of the big things  that I think has really surprised me is  how absolutely amazingly difficult the  whole breastfeeding thing actually is I  think that we tend to position  breastfeeding as being some super  natural innate thing that women are just  supposed to be able to get but I have  found that it is so far from natural or  innate in fact it's probably being one  of the hardest things I've ever done in  my life so I think all moms have their  own struggle when it comes to  breastfeeding for us there were a number  of things that were stacked against us  one my baby came out and was super  congested so the LC or the lactation  consultant right away said oh my gosh  that baby's not gonna want to eat  because they are just so clogged up he  literally sounded like an English  Bulldog that's how bad it was number two  I had PCOS and a postpartum hemorrhage  both of those things apparently can  delay and reduce breast milk supply so  that was a challenge for sure and then  number three my little one had both a  lip and a tongue type and if you're not  familiar what that is you're like well  what the heck does that mean basically  stick your finger in your mouth  underneath your tongue you'll feel a  little piece of skin there that's  connecting your tongue to the bottom of  your mouth  well my babies is really really tight or  at least it was so we had to have that  kind of cut out or removed in order for  him to be able to move his tongue up and  down which is a really important  movement during breastfeeding so I  basically had both a issue of reduced  supply and also  just a really bad latch so breastfeeding  was pretty much impossible it was  painful I wasn't able to have enough  milk to feed my very very very hungry  hippo baby and it was just absolutely  exhausting I mean as a registered  dietician I we've been all trained to  believe that fed is best and I  wholeheartedly do believe it you know if  you were to ask me or if I was speaking  to another mom I would say oh my gosh  you've got to do whatever you can to  make sure that your baby is being fed  enough and and that means supplementing  and that's great you know but when it  happened to me I felt so defeated I felt  like my body let me down and then I was  letting down my baby if I became  completely obsessed and the first night  I remember we we supplemented quite a  lot and and every night after that it  you know the ratio started to to go down  and down it down but it for me it was  still just so so disheartening that even  an ounce of formula had to go into my  baby because I felt like my body should  be able to do this all you know I  shouldn't be having these problems and  that I should be able to provide this  one thing for my baby and and I just  felt like I was inadequate  I feel like you know there's there's two  things happening here  one is the delivery of breast milk  itself and that's being seen as you know  superior form of nourishment for our  babies and then two is the idea of  actually breastfeeding and that that's  posted ly able to kind of create a  really important bond between mom and  baby and I was feeling at both of those  I felt like I wasn't able to give my  child exclusively breast milk I was  pumping around the clock like literally  every hour on the hour I was attached to  that pump in order to get some breast  milk to feed my child but that meant I  would have to bottle feed because we had  such a bad latch that I just wasn't able  to get him on the breast so I just  remember you know the first few times we  left the house and of course with  bottles in tow I was feeding my child  with a bottle even though it was Ashley  breast milk I felt like every single  other mom around me was judging me was  judging me for not putting that baby on  the breast because we we do talk about  it all the time and I can't tell you how  many random strangers that I saw me on  the street or people who I barely know  or friends or family who would say are  you breastfeeding they seems like  everybody asked me you know aside from  how are you doing and what's-his-name  the leg the next question was always are  you breastfeeding and I you know before  I gave I gave birth that would have been  a question I never even thought about  but in that moment I felt so anxious and  ashamed and guilty that no I wasn't  breastfeeding  and and you know I was made to feel  really bad about that so looking back at  you know those first two days especially  now after having spoken to so many of my  friends and colleagues who had similar  issues I'm starting to realize that you  know we're all in this together and and  we're all just trying to do the best  that we can and we don't need to feel  like a failure and supplementing even  exclusively feel like feeding formula is  absolutely okay because bed ultimately  is best we want our babies to be  nourished regardless of what that fuel  may be whether it's breast milk or  formula or a combination of both and the  other really important thing is that you  know it's not just about what's best for  the baby but also what's kind of best  for mom - what's best for mom sanity I  know a lot of women who've told me that  by the time that they finally kind of  gave up on breastfeeding and allowed  themselves to have that break they felt  like their whole world opened up that  they they were just able to get out more  they were able to be full feel more  comfortable they had a better  relationship with their partner they had  a better relationship with their baby  because they weren't stressing all day  and all night  about making sure that every single  little drop of fuel that went to their  baby was from their breast and it's a  really hard place to get to but I'm I'm  starting to feel like a lot more at ease  just being good enough with with you  know the hand I be dealt  so I got so much feedback after I  started to open up about my own  struggles with breastfeeding and I think  before I gave birth you know the only  role models I really had were my own mom  who I knew breastfed both my sister and  I and a handful my friends who had kids  and you know I would watch my friends  just so effortlessly put that baby on  their boob and go about their own kind  of conversation they didn't pump they  didn't have to bottle-feed they didn't  have to supplement they don't have to do  any of these things that I felt like I  was like struggling and failing because  I had to do so after reaching out and  and all of you guys at home by blogging  about this on Abbey's kitchen and seeing  the the crazy amount of feedback I got  from so many of you moms who had similar  struggles I just felt so much more  comfortable knowing that you know it's  okay it's okay to to not be perfect it's  okay that this is going to be maybe a  long long journey it's okay that I just  don't innately get this and baby and I  are latched on no problem  no supplementing right from the get-go  and I was really surprised I mean I was  surprised that so many of us are  struggling in silence when you know this  not a good thing seems to be something  that's talked about quite a lot I'm  surprised that I I never really got this  sentiment from from the circles that I  meant it's like nobody wants to talk  about the fact that they feel and  Hateley like they're failing their  infant when reality is we're all just  doing the best we can so as a dietitian  my inclination was to look at the food  what foods can I eat in order to  increase my milk supply and there are so  many blog posts out there if you  searched the internet on a lot of the  mommy blogs and all of them state a very  specific list of foods that you want to  eat in order to increase your milk  supply so things like oatmeal and fennel  and fenugreek and brewers yeast and  asparagus apricots almonds flaxseed  barley there's a lot of these foods that  that people talk about that apparently  increase your supply having said that  when I actually looked at the literature  I found that there's just not a lot of  studies out there that have been done to  prove that these foods actually are  correlated with a boost in milk supply  that doesn't mean that they don't we  just don't really know for sure but  having said that I would say that ate  these foods are really tasty anyways and  B these foods are really nutritious for  you they're nutrient dense and when  you're breastfeeding or or nursing your  nutrient needs are through the roof as  high as a thousand extra calories above  your baseline so including a lot of  these foods in your diet is a really  good easy way to ensure that you're  getting the nutrition that you actually  need in order to support your baby and I  can tell you from my own experience the  days that I eat more and I really kind  of like have to think about eating  enough and making sure that I'm not so  busy that I forget to have lunch or  forget to have breakfast those are the  days where I get the most milk and that  is evidence of that for me at least  but I think the most important thing  that I've learned through this whole  breastfeeding experience is to be kind  to myself and you know so many other  moms out there are experiencing the  exact same thing or very similar  challenges so if we reach out and talk  to one another about it and support one  another you know we feel so much better  so much more empowered and so much more  at ease knowing that you know we're just  doing the best we can at this whole mom  thing and that is good enough so guys  thank you so much for watching this  video if you enjoyed it be sure to give  it the thumbs up leave me a comment  below if you feel comfortable telling me  a little bit about some of your  experiences with breastfeeding was easy  for you was it a challenge what did you  find actually helped you with your  supply and of course subscribe to the  channel I'll see you next time on Abby's  kitchen bye  [Music]  you

